== History ==

{{main|Portblanc#History}}

The area of present-day Cala Tranquilla was originally inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]], who maintained fishing communities along the Shendan coast. Volisanian explorers reached the harbour in 1526 and established a mission in 1568, marking the beginning of permanent colonial settlement. The settlement was designated the provincial capital of Tramuntana within the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]], serving as the administrative centre for the northern highland region.

During the colonial period, the comarca's economy was based on cattle ranching on the dry Tramuntana savanna and maritime trade through the port of Portblanc. The [[Birú people|Birú]] population of the surrounding highlands was devastated by conquest, disease, and forced labour, with survivors retreating into the remote northern highlands.

Following the Treaty of San Llàtzer (1899), which transferred Sant Cristòfor and the Balboa Canal to Zong control, Portblanc became the principal western port of the truncated Balboan republic. The [[Portblanc Military Academy]] was established in 1908, becoming one of the most significant institutions in the comarca. The rump state period saw the comarca's population grow as maritime trade was rerouted through Portblanc.

The reunification of 1973 reintegrated the canal territories into the Balboan state, reducing Portblanc's strategic importance as the rump state's principal western port. The comarca nevertheless benefited from national infrastructure investment, including the extension of the expressway network and the development of offshore oil fields in the 1980s. The [[Portblanc school desegregation crisis]] of 1982–1984 was among the most significant racial integration conflicts of the post-reunification era, leading to the dissolution of comarca-level education control.

In 2024, the [[2024 Portblanc fires]] destroyed approximately 1,200 houses and caused eight deaths in the northwestern districts of the comarca, where residential development had expanded into the dry grasslands of the coastal Tramuntana range. In February 2026, the [[2026 Portblanc fires]] destroyed 36 houses in the eastern districts.

== Economy ==

{{main|Portblanc#Economy}}

The economy of Cala Tranquilla is dominated by the city of [[Portblanc]] and is centred on port activities, government services, and the [[Portblanc Military Academy]]. As the seat of the provincial government, the comarca hosts the offices of the Tramuntana Provincial Assembly and the provincial administration, providing a concentration of public sector employment that distinguishes it from the predominantly rural comarcas of the interior.

The [[Port of Portblanc]] handles regional cargo, including agricultural products from the Tramuntana highlands, petroleum from the offshore oil fields, and aquaculture products from the coastal zone. Offshore oil extraction operations in the Shendan coastal waters are a significant economic activity. Aquaculture, particularly shrimp farming, has developed in the mangrove nurseries of the coastal zone.

The comarca is the poorest provincial-level urban centre in Balboa, reflecting the broader poverty of Tramuntana Province, which has a GDP per capita of approximately $2,400 and a poverty rate of 42.6 per cent. The concentration of public sector employment, military institutions, and port activity in Portblanc makes the comarca comparatively more prosperous than the surrounding rural comarcas, though it remains significantly less affluent than the comarcas of the Estret-Jala corridor.

In 2026, [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] (NTE) opened a data center in Portblanc with a capacity of 3 MW, and the associated gas-fired power station represents the most significant private sector investment in the comarca in recent years.

== Demographics ==

Cala Tranquilla had a population of 840,000 in 2026, making it the most populous comarca in Tramuntana Province and the most densely populated, with a density of 653.2/km². The population is overwhelmingly concentrated in the city of [[Portblanc]].

The ethnic composition reflects the comarca's history as a northern highland provincial capital. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] constitute the largest single group, concentrated in the city and the coastal towns, descended from Volisanian settlers and from the Illypnian immigrants who arrived under the [[Redilution]] and [[Birthing movement|Birthing]] programmes that targeted the northern highlands. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are a smaller presence than in the central and southern provinces, though a community exists in the city. The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population of the surrounding highlands maintains a presence in the city, though the principal Birú communities are concentrated in the remote northern comarcas.

== Government ==

{{main|Portblanc#Government and politics}}

The comarca is administered by a comarca commissioner, with the comarca seat located in [[Portblanc]]. As the provincial capital, Portblanc hosts the [[Tramuntana Provincial Assembly]] and the offices of the provincial government, including the governor of Tramuntana Province.

The [[Portblanc High Court]] serves as the principal court of first instance for the Cala Tranquilla comarca and exercises appellate jurisdiction over lower courts in the surrounding comarcas. The court gained national prominence in 1988 as the venue for the ''[[Balboa v. Riera i Sola]]'' case.

The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] maintains a regional office in Portblanc, one of four regional offices serving the country, covering Tramuntana and Nurra provinces.

== Transport ==

{{main|Portblanc#Transport}}

Cala Tranquilla is a regional transport hub for Tramuntana Province, with road, rail, air, and maritime connections converging on the city of Portblanc.

Four national expressways pass through the comarca: [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]], the principal north–south artery, runs from the Jala border in the south through Portblanc to the far northern comarcas; [[Expressway 2 (Balboa)|Expressway 2]] crosses the Yalabirga mountains from Portblanc on the Shendan coast to [[Novara]] on the Aethiopian coast; [[Expressway 8 (Balboa)|Expressway 8]] runs east–west through the northern comarcas, connecting the Nurra border region to the Shendan coast via Portblanc; and [[Expressway 116 (Balboa)|Expressway 116]] is a short connector between Portblanc and the central transport hub of [[La Cruïlla (comarca)|La Cruïlla]].

The [[Portblanc Line]], operated by [[Ferrocarrils de l'Istme]] (FI), is the principal railway serving the comarca, running from [[Sant Bart]] in [[Jala Province]] through the southern comarcas to Portblanc. The line is primarily a freight corridor carrying agricultural products and petroleum, but operates a limited passenger service. A long-distance rail service connects Portblanc to [[Sant Cristòfor]] via the Sant Cristòfor–Portblanc Line.

A streetcar system operates within Portblanc, comprising three lines. The system was originally constructed in 1934 as a single line connecting the harbour to the city centre, electrified and expanded to two lines in 1952, and modernised in 1998 with the construction of a third line and the introduction of low-floor trams.

[[Portblanc Airport]] (IATA: PBL, ICAO: PBIB) is a domestic jet-capable airport located on the Shendan coast within the comarca. The airport is served by [[Aeroístmus]] and [[RapidAir (Balboa)|RapidAir]] from their respective hubs, with onward service to [[Sant Jorge Airport|Sant Jorge]] on the Aeroístmus route. The airport handled approximately 480,000 passengers in 2025.

The [[Port of Portblanc]] is a harbour on the Shendan coast, handling regional cargo including agricultural products, petroleum, and aquaculture products. A fishing harbour supports the marine fisheries that operate from the city.

== Education ==

{{main|Universitat de Portblanc|Portblanc school desegregation crisis}}

The [[Universitat de Portblanc]] is a public university located in the comarca. Originally established as the University College of Portblanc in 1964 during the rump state period, the institution was granted full university status in 1978 following reunification. The university offers programmes in agriculture, marine sciences, education, public administration, and the humanities, and serves as the principal provider of higher education in Tramuntana Province.

The [[Portblanc school desegregation crisis]] of 1982–1984 was a pivotal event in the history of education in Balboa. The Portblanc school board voted in 1982 to dissolve the public school system and replace it with government-funded "private" schools free to set their own admissions criteria, in an attempt to circumvent the desegregation requirements of the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. A national inquisition (1983–1984) found Portblanc in violation of the Guarantees, and the Ferrer administration ordered the restoration of the public school system in 1984. The crisis led to the dissolution of comarca-level education control and the establishment of the current shared model of education governance.

